<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/estimate-laptop.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="626" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li><li aria-current="page">Decoding Your Carrier’s Estimate, One Line Item at a Time</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Claim advice · 8 min read</p><h1>Decoding Your Carrier’s Estimate, One Line Item at a Time</h1><p class="lede">A plain guide to the parts of an Xactimate-style estimate and the omissions that most often shrink a Mississippi property claim.</p></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Mississippi Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-11-09">November 9, 2026</time> · 8 min read</p><h2>Why the estimate is the heart of the claim</h2><p>Almost every property claim in Mississippi comes down to a document: the carrier’s estimate. It lists what the adjuster thinks was damaged, how much of it, and what it should cost to fix. If an item isn’t on the estimate, it usually isn’t paid. If a quantity is short, the payment is short.</p><p>Most carriers and adjusters use Xactimate or similar software that prices repairs from regional price lists. The format can look intimidating, with dense pages of codes and numbers, but once you know what each column means it becomes much easier to see where your claim may be underpaid.</p><h2>Anatomy of a single line</h2><p>Each line item typically has the same building blocks:</p><ul><li><strong>Description.</strong> What the work is, such as “Remove laminated comp. shingle rfg. w/out felt” or “Drywall, 1/2 inch, hung, taped, floated, ready for paint.”</li><li><strong>Quantity and unit.</strong> How much, in squares (SQ), square feet (SF), linear feet (LF) or each (EA).</li><li><strong>Unit price.</strong> The cost per unit, usually combining labor and materials.</li><li><strong>Tax.</strong> Sales tax on materials, often shown as its own column or line.</li><li><strong>RCV.</strong> Replacement cost value, the full price for the line before depreciation.</li><li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> The amount subtracted for age and condition, sometimes marked as recoverable or non-recoverable.</li><li><strong>ACV.</strong> Actual cash value, RCV minus depreciation.</li></ul><p>Estimates are usually organized by area: roof, each exterior elevation, each room inside, and sometimes detached structures and contents. Each area should have its own measurements.</p><h2>Checking quantities and prices</h2><h3>Quantities</h3><p>Quantity errors are among the most common problems. On a roof, check that every slope was measured, that the waste factor fits the roof’s complexity, and that ridge, hip, valley, starter and drip edge lengths are included. A roof square is 100 square feet; our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/roof-pitch-squares-calculator/">roof pitch and squares calculator</a> can help you check the math.</p><p>Inside, compare room dimensions to your own tape measurements. A ceiling stain might require painting the whole ceiling, and often the walls, for an even finish.</p><h3>Unit prices</h3><p>Price lists are updated regularly, but after a big storm local labor and material costs in places like Hattiesburg or the Coast can run ahead of the list. Compare the estimate’s unit prices to your contractor’s bid and ask the adjuster which price list and date were used.</p><div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Read the summary page, too</b><p>The last page usually shows totals for RCV, depreciation, ACV, the deductible and prior payments. Make sure the deductible matches your declarations page and that every earlier check is counted correctly.</p></div><h2>Overhead, profit, depreciation and tax</h2><p>Below the line items, a few adjustments can swing the total:</p><ul><li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> When a job needs a general contractor to coordinate several trades, such as roofing, drywall, painting and electrical, many estimates include overhead and profit, often figured as a percentage. Carriers sometimes leave it off smaller or single-trade jobs.</li><li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Check the age and condition the adjuster assumed. Depreciating labor, or using a roof age that doesn’t match your records, can reduce the first payment. Whether labor depreciation is allowed depends on your policy and the law, so ask about it in writing.</li><li><strong>Recoverable versus non-recoverable.</strong> On replacement cost policies, much of the depreciation is usually paid back after repairs. Our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/holdback-recovery-calculator/">holdback recovery calculator</a> helps you estimate that amount.</li><li><strong>Tax.</strong> Confirm sales tax is applied to materials and that it follows the estimate’s totals.</li></ul><h2>Items that often go missing</h2><p>When we review Mississippi estimates, these items are often absent or understated:</p><ul><li>Drip edge, starter strip and ice and water shield where code or the manufacturer requires them</li><li>Steep and high-roof charges for roofs that need extra labor and safety gear</li><li>Removal of extra shingle layers, and replacement of damaged decking</li><li>Code upgrades under ordinance or law coverage</li><li>Matching siding, flooring or cabinets when an exact replacement isn’t available</li><li>Detach and reset of gutters, satellite dishes, solar panels and fixtures</li><li>Content manipulation: moving furniture out and back in to do the work</li><li>Permits, dumpsters, debris haul-off and final cleaning</li></ul><h2>A worked look at one roof line</h2><p><strong>Illustrative example:</strong> an estimate lists “Laminated comp. shingle rfg. w/ felt” at 24.33 SQ and $310.00 per square, for $7,542.30 before tax. Your contractor measured 27.67 SQ including a waste factor suited to a cut-up roof with several valleys. At the same unit price, the gap is about $1,035 on that single line, and related lines such as felt, starter and ridge usually move with it. Small quantity differences like this, repeated across a roof and several rooms, are how two estimates for the same house end up thousands of dollars apart.</p><p>When you find a difference, write down the line number, the carrier’s figure, your figure and the reason, such as a missed slope or a measurement that doesn’t match. That short list becomes the backbone of a supplement request, and if the claim later goes to appraisal, it gives both appraisers a clear starting point.</p><h2>Turning your notes into a response</h2><p>Once you have marked up the estimate, put your questions in writing to the adjuster, item by item. Attach photos, measurements and your contractor’s estimate. Many differences are resolved this way through a supplement.</p><p>If you still disagree on the amount after that, appraisal may be available under your policy. Our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> compares the carrier’s estimate to the actual damage, and the <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a> can help you see whether the gap is worth pursuing. Remember that appraisal sets the amount of loss, not coverage. For coverage questions, talk to a Mississippi attorney.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/wind-vs-water-after-katrina/"><small>Guide</small><span>Wind vs. Water Claims in Mississippi: What Katrina Still Teaches</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/mississippi-appraisal-clause-guide/"><small>Guide</small><span>The Mississippi Appraisal Clause, Read Line by Line</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel-card" href="/claim-advice/coast-hurricane-deductibles-explained/"><small>Guide</small><span>Coast Hurricane Deductibles: The Math Behind a Named Storm Claim</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm CT</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">Appraisal roadmap</a></li><li><a href="/questions-answered/">Questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">Insurance claim terms</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li></ul></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Free calculators